orlds  in lanthamosis second film dog tooth a  father constructs a false reality for  his grown children  that he's trapped at home and isolated  from the outside world  [Music]  in this alternate reality words for  things that exist  outside the family's home are redefined  to refer to things  within their home  their world has its own weird rules  norms and definitions  that don't make any sense to us as  outsiders and yet the children  blindly accept these things because it's  all they know when the oldest sibling  throws the sun's toy airplane outside  the fence  the sun doesn't try to walk out and get  it himself instead he asks the father to  use the car to pick it up  at this point in the film it hasn't been  explained that there's a rule that the  children cannot go outside the fence  but we infer the existence of that rule  because we see  the sun obeying it in the lobster nimik  and to some extent the killing of a  sacred deer  lanthamos is playing the role of the  father in dogtooth  as the director he constructs worlds  with strange norms and rules  that have an absurd logic and he reveals  those rules to us  through how the characters respect and  obey them  in dogtooth we understand that the  father has arbitrarily defined these  rules  that they can be broken but in the  lobster the killing of a sacred deer and  nimik  we don't get to see the origin of the  rules that define those film's alternate  realities  instead lanthamos imbues these worlds  with a sincerity through the use of  visual cues  like this slow zoom lobster because  lobsters live  for over 100 years are blue-blooded  like aristocrats  and stay fertile all their lives  in the killing of a sacred deer  otherwise benign interactions like  martin the young man  visiting the family gain almost a  metaphysical eeriness  through the same movement  good afternoon you must be martin that's  right  you must be anna that's right in typical  film grammar a camera movement like this  is usually used to indicate that  something of significance is happening  lanthamos unsettles us by using this  move when what's significant  about the moment isn't obvious martin  does turn out to have a sort of  metaphysical power  and on the subway in nimik this odd but  benign interaction does cause  something strange to happen in the same  way lanthamos suggests the existence of  a larger structure of rules in his  worlds  through how the characters behave even  if we don't understand or learn about  those rules  his cinematography suggests that  something significant is happening in  these moments  even if we don't understand exactly what  this approach to filming these absurd  worlds lends a sort of seriousness and  significance to the world's absurd  rules the characters in the world  respect the rules and even the film  itself  understands and projects the  significance of these occurrences  the deadpan delivery of lines extends  into  an almost deadpan delivery of the film  itself  makes watching these stories an  incredibly strange unsettling and  sometimes humorous experience  as the audience we may feel like we're  missing something why don't the  characters push back  why do they accept the absurd reality  they're inside children please  children tell your mother who the real  father is  how should we know we're just kids  don't they see its absurdity but these  characters aren't passively resigned to  the world around them because they are  apathetic and uncaring they are resigned  because they don't believe these rules  are arbitrary  then the procedure changes according to  the animal when it's chosen  that makes total sense we can see this  reflected in how the characters in the  lobster talk about people who have been  turned into animals  my mother entered the hotel but didn't  make it and was turned into a wolf  they speak about it like a death  something that is outside of their  control  the fact that they fail to even question  these rules and norms suggests that they  believe  they don't have the power to change them  that their physical or metaphysical laws  of the universe  and not just arbitrary social norms a  wolf and a penguin could never live  together  nor could a camel and a hippopotamus  that would be absurd  think about it i know you're in there  the main characters in lanthamosa's  films usually do try to push back  against the horrifying outcomes of their  strange reality  but they can only imagine doing so  within the larger framework of how their  world works  in the lobster david fights back against  being turned into an animal just because  he's single he doesn't want that outcome  but it doesn't even occur to him to  question  the basis of relationships in his world  i'm very happy  because we have a new couple they met  just two days ago but they are very much  in love and perfectly suited  they both have the same problem with  their noses they bleed quite suddenly  the fact that a couple's compatibility  is based entirely on small arbitrary  similarities  his nose doesn't really bleed on its own  he bangs it against a wall  or a piece of hardwood or something like  that he sees this  as just the way things are you two are  not suited  something inherent to the world that  cannot be rebelled against  in nimick the father pushes back he  doesn't want this woman to  invade his family and replace him but he  ultimately accepts the outcome  determined by the bizarre test  that the family conducts in the killing  of a sacred deer the family doesn't want  to slowly die  but they only fight for their individual  survival within the rules of the curse  or diagnosis that's been given them  i believe the most logical thing no  matter how harsh this may sound is to  kill a child  because we can have another child unlike  dog tooth where we see the source of the  rules  and know them to be false and arbitrary  in the lobster mimic and the killing of  the sacred deer  we don't really know who's making the  rules the characters follow  so we have to wonder are they formed by  society  the physical universe or a metaphysical  reality  this is the core of lanthamosa's  cultural and social commentary in these  films  do you understand it's metaphorical  my example it's a metaphor  i mean creating worlds with different  norms and rules ones that seem absurd to  us  he forces us to examine the norms and  rules that shape our own world  we're forced to wonder what might be  arbitrary ideas that we accept simply  because it's what we've been taught  and what society upholds as normal and  what might be  a deeper more fundamental aspect of  our world something that we truly cannot  escape  it's through this reshaping of reality  that lancimos gives us new perspectives  on family  romantic relationships the  doctor-patient relationship  and marriage redefining words or ideas  to break us out of a tired perspective  so we can glimpse what absurdities  in our own world we might be blindly  accepting  i think understanding the lobster or any  of lantermos's other bizarre works in  this category  isn't a project of understanding what  the rules are and where they came from  and why things are the way they are in  that world but of  understanding the characters  relationships to those rules  and norms and what that reflects about  our own world  if you want to watch nimik lanthimosa's  newest film the short just premiered  exclusively on mubi  thank you to mubi for collaborating on  this essay and giving me early access to  nimik and allowing me to use footage  from the short in this video  mubi is a curated streaming service  showing hand-picked films from around  the globe  right now lanthamosa's breakout film  dogtooth is also  available as part of the movie library  the work of foreign and independent  filmmakers like lanzimos are mainstays  of movies library of films  so if you want to discover more work in  this vein mubi  is an excellent way to do that you can  watch nimik dogt